Use LighthouseCI GitHub Action to automatically check website quality, SEO, and improvement suggestions

![I’m sure every developer is familiar with this.](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1036/format:webp/1*ierDQZJHeTp_FVCystFNJA.png)

In modern front-end development, website performance, SEO, and accessibility are becoming increasingly important. Using [Lighthouse](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/lighthouse/overview) for testing has become a common practice, and the official site provides detailed, step-by-step guides to help you monitor them.

Therefore, the following will not focus on explaining the meaning of each metric or how to improve them, but will instead focus on Lighthouse CI — specifically, how I use [Lighthouse CI](https://github.com/GoogleChrome/lighthouse-ci/blob/main/docs/getting-started.md) and GitHub Actions in a Next.js project to automatically generate Lighthouse reports for every Pull Request, along with my personal insights.

For those who are not familiar with CI, [you can check this out.](https://github.com/GoogleChrome/lighthouse-ci/blob/main/docs/introduction-to-ci.md)👈🏻👈🏻

![This image clearly illustrates the Lighthouse CI workflow](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1400/format:webp/1*t4NBVCoNXl2laW3g6noRdg.png)

🚀 **Why do this?**

Why do it matters: time and energy are traded, so know the value. We’re an e-commerce platform, and SEO is critical. Manually running scores while shipping new features is tedious; I want an easier way to monitor (or I’m just lazy?).

Also, tracking metric score changes for every commit is very appealing to me.

